# Claude Code driver for the agent-teammates scripts. The coordinator reads the two declarations below (through the
# harness reference); the shared scripts don't source this file; retask.sh reads its `SEND=` line. Each operation below is an executable in this
# directory, run by the shared scripts through `drv <op> <args>` (team-env.zsh). A missing operation makes `drv`
# return 9 with a message on stderr, which the callers map to exit 5. Operations must never exit 9 themselves.
# LIVE_MESSAGING: a live teammate can be sent new work, so the coordinator keeps its session open between rounds.
# SEND=tool: send is a tool operation (SendMessage to the teammate's session name), so there is no `send` executable.
LIVE_MESSAGING=1
SEND=tool
#
# Operations. <team-dir> is the run directory; the driver reads its team.env (through team-env.zsh) for its own
# settings. Every operation exits 0 on success; a non-zero exit is the shared script's exit code.
#
#   launch <team-dir> <name> <workdir> <model> <prompt-file> <read-only: 0|1>
#       Starts a background session named <name> in <workdir> (an existing directory) on <model>, told to read
#       <prompt-file>, and opens a viewer pane if team.env asks for one. On success prints line 1 `<session-id> <viewer-ref|->`,
#       then the lines to show the engineer after the "launched" line. Failure prints a message and exits
#       1 (sessions couldn't be listed, or the session started with no id), 2 (no background session can run;
#       the last line starts `NO_BG:` and names what to do instead), or 5 (a live session already has <name>).
#   status <team-dir>
#       Prints one line per known session: `<session-id> <status> <state> <last-activity-epoch|->`, `-` for an
#       empty value; exits 3, printing nothing, when the sessions can't be listed. Only <session-id>, <state> and
#       <last-activity-epoch> are read (watch-teammates.sh); <status> is unused and may be `-`. <last-activity-epoch>
#       is when the session last did anything, however the driver finds out (Claude: the transcript's mtime, looked up
#       for the sessions in teammates.tsv only); `-` when unknown, and STALL then never fires for that session. The watchdog acts on <state>, and a session with
#       no row counts as `missing`:
#         working   the session is mid-turn; STALL fires when its last activity is older than STALL_MIN
#         blocked   its turn ended, or it waits on a prompt; two polls in a row fire BLOCKED
#         done      its turn ended normally; counts like blocked (no .done marker yet)
#         anything else   resets the BLOCKED count and fires nothing but STALE
#         stopped | failed | missing   the session is gone; GONE fires. A harness whose process exits when a turn
#                   ends must report that as `blocked` or `done`, never `stopped` or `failed`.
#   tail <team-dir> <label> <session-id> <n>
#       Prints the session's last <n> tool calls and its latest message as plain text, after a `transcript: ...` line.
#       <label> is the name or id the engineer typed, for the message when nothing is found (exit 3).
#   stop <team-dir> <session-id>
#       Stops the session without removing anything it created. Exits 0 if it was running, non-zero if not.
#   send <team-dir> <session-id> <message-file>
#       Reserved for drivers that declare SEND=script (read by retask.sh). Gives the session <message-file> as its next job,
#       resuming it if its process has exited. Returns once the job has started, without waiting for the turn to end, and
#       exits non-zero if it couldn't start the job. Drivers with SEND=tool have no send executable; the coordinator sends
#       through the harness's own tool.
#   advisor-probe <team-dir> <model>
#       Prints `ADVISOR ON <model>` or `ADVISOR OFF <model>: <reason>` as its last line; exits 0 either way.
#       A driver with no advisor-probe makes team-preflight print `ADVISOR n/a (<harness>)`.
#   preflight <run-dir>      (optional)
#       The harness's own runtime checks: prints OK / WARN / FAIL lines, exits 1 if any is FAIL.
#
# Per-session state. A driver may keep its own files per session under <team-dir> (name them after the driver,
# e.g. <team-dir>/<harness>-<session-id>). `launch` must record there whatever the driver's re-tasking (send) needs
# that teammates.tsv doesn't hold: the read-only flag, the model and the workdir.
